DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The library routines described on this page provide the application ac‐
     cess to the system network configuration database, _/_e_t_c_/_n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g.  The
     ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg() function returns a pointer to the current entry in the
     netconfig database, formatted as a _s_t_r_u_c_t _n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g.  Successive calls
     will return successive netconfig entries in the netconfig database.  The
     ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg() function can be used to search the entire netconfig file.
     The ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg() function returns NULL at the end of the file.  The
     _h_a_n_d_l_e_p argument is the handle obtained through sseettnneettccoonnffiigg().

     A call to sseettnneettccoonnffiigg() has the effect of “binding” to or “rewinding”
     the netconfig database.  The sseettnneettccoonnffiigg() function must be called be‐
     fore the first call to ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg() and may be called at any other
     time.  The sseettnneettccoonnffiigg() function need not be called before a call to
     ggeettnneettccoonnffiiggeenntt().  The sseettnneettccoonnffiigg() function returns a unique handle
     to be used by ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg().

     The eennddnneettccoonnffiigg() function should be called when processing is complete
     to release resources for reuse.  The _h_a_n_d_l_e_p argument is the handle ob‐
     tained through sseettnneettccoonnffiigg().  Programmers should be aware, however,
     that the last call to eennddnneettccoonnffiigg() frees all memory allocated by
     ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg() for the _s_t_r_u_c_t _n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g data structure.  The
     eennddnneettccoonnffiigg() function may not be called before sseettnneettccoonnffiigg().

     The ggeettnneettccoonnffiiggeenntt() function returns a pointer to the netconfig struc‐
     ture corresponding to _n_e_t_i_d.  It returns NULL if _n_e_t_i_d is invalid (that
     is, does not name an entry in the netconfig database).

     The ffrreeeenneettccoonnffiiggeenntt() function frees the netconfig structure pointed to
     by _n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g_p (previously returned by ggeettnneettccoonnffiiggeenntt()).

     The nncc__ppeerrrroorr() function prints a message to the standard error indicat‐
     ing why any of the above routines failed.  The message is prepended with
     the string _m_s_g and a colon.  A newline character is appended at the end
     of the message.

     The nncc__ssppeerrrroorr() function is similar to nncc__ppeerrrroorr() but instead of send‐
     ing the message to the standard error, will return a pointer to a string
     that contains the error message.

     The nncc__ppeerrrroorr() and nncc__ssppeerrrroorr() functions can also be used with the
     NETPATH access routines defined in getnetpath(3).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The sseettnneettccoonnffiigg() function returns a unique handle to be used by
     ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg().  In the case of an error, sseettnneettccoonnffiigg() returns NULL and
     nncc__ppeerrrroorr() or nncc__ssppeerrrroorr() can be used to print the reason for failure.

     The ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg() function returns a pointer to the current entry in the
     netconfig database, formatted as a _s_t_r_u_c_t _n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g.  The ggeettnneettccoonnffiigg()
     function returns NULL at the end of the file, or upon failure.

     The eennddnneettccoonnffiigg() function returns 0 on success and -1 on failure (for
     example, if sseettnneettccoonnffiigg() was not called previously).

     On success, ggeettnneettccoonnffiiggeenntt() returns a pointer to the _s_t_r_u_c_t _n_e_t_c_o_n_f_i_g
     structure corresponding to _n_e_t_i_d; otherwise it returns NULL.

     The nncc__ssppeerrrroorr() function returns a pointer to a buffer which contains
     the error message string.  This buffer is overwritten on each call.  In
     multithreaded applications, this buffer is implemented as thread-specific
     data.

